You can restructure your architecture so that nginx handles routing while Spring Cloud Gateway focuses solely on authentication. Here's how to implement this:

## Architecture Overview

```
Client → nginx (routing) → Spring Cloud Gateway (auth only) → Backend Services
```

## Implementation Steps

### 1. **Modify Spring Cloud Gateway to Auth-Only Mode**

Remove routing logic and keep only authentication/authorization:

```java
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {
    
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/health", "/actuator/**").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt())
            // or session-based auth
            .sessionManagement(session -> session
                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.IF_REQUIRED)
            );
        
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

### 2. **Create an Authentication Endpoint**

Add a controller that validates credentials and returns auth tokens/headers:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/auth")
public class AuthController {
    
    @PostMapping("/validate")
    public ResponseEntity<AuthResponse> validateAuth(HttpServletRequest request) {
        // Your existing auth logic
        Authentication auth = SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication();
        
        if (auth != null && auth.isAuthenticated()) {
            // Return auth info that nginx can forward
            return ResponseEntity.ok(AuthResponse.builder()
                .userId(auth.getName())
                .roles(auth.getAuthorities().stream()
                    .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
                    .collect(Collectors.toList()))
                .authenticated(true)
                .build());
        }
        
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).build();
    }
    
    // Optional: Token generation endpoint
    @PostMapping("/login")
    public ResponseEntity<TokenResponse> login(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        // Authenticate and generate token
        String token = generateToken(request);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(new TokenResponse(token));
    }
}
```

### 3. **Configure nginx for Routing with Auth Subrequest**

Use nginx `auth_request` module to validate with Spring Cloud Gateway:

```nginx
upstream spring_auth {
    server localhost:8080;  # Your Spring Cloud Gateway
}

upstream service1 {
    server localhost:8081;
}

upstream service2 {
    server localhost:8082;
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name your-domain.com;

    # Auth validation endpoint
    location = /auth/validate {
        internal;
        proxy_pass http://spring_auth;
        proxy_pass_request_body off;
        proxy_set_header Content-Length "";
        proxy_set_header X-Original-URI $request_uri;
        proxy_set_header X-Original-Method $request_method;
    }

    # Route to Service 1
    location /api/service1/ {
        auth_request /auth/validate;
        
        # Pass auth headers from validation response
        auth_request_set $user_id $upstream_http_x_user_id;
        auth_request_set $user_roles $upstream_http_x_user_roles;
        
        proxy_set_header X-User-Id $user_id;
        proxy_set_header X-User-Roles $user_roles;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        
        proxy_pass http://service1/;
    }

    # Route to Service 2
    location /api/service2/ {
        auth_request /auth/validate;
        
        auth_request_set $user_id $upstream_http_x_user_id;
        auth_request_set $user_roles $upstream_http_x_user_roles;
        
        proxy_set_header X-User-Id $user_id;
        proxy_set_header X-User-Roles $user_roles;
        
        proxy_pass http://service2/;
    }
}
```

### 4. **Update Auth Validation to Return Headers**

Modify your auth controller to return user info in response headers:

```java
@PostMapping("/validate")
public ResponseEntity<Void> validateAuth(HttpServletRequest request) {
    Authentication auth = SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication();
    
    if (auth != null && auth.isAuthenticated()) {
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.add("X-User-Id", auth.getName());
        headers.add("X-User-Roles", auth.getAuthorities().stream()
            .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
            .collect(Collectors.joining(",")));
        
        return ResponseEntity.ok().headers(headers).build();
    }
    
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).build();
}
```

## Alternative: Token-Based Approach (Simpler)

If you prefer, use JWT tokens and let nginx just forward them:

### nginx config:
```nginx
location /api/service1/ {
    # Just forward the Authorization header
    proxy_set_header Authorization $http_authorization;
    proxy_pass http://service1/;
}
```

### Backend services validate tokens themselves:
```java
// In each microservice
@Configuration
public class JwtSecurityConfig {
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http.oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2.jwt());
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

## Benefits of This Approach

* **nginx**: Fast routing, load balancing, SSL termination
* **Spring Cloud Gateway**: Centralized authentication/authorization
* **Separation of concerns**: Each component does what it's best at
* **Performance**: nginx is more efficient for routing than Java

## Migration Path

1. Keep existing Spring Cloud Gateway running
2. Set up nginx with auth_request pointing to your gateway
3. Test thoroughly
4. Gradually move routes from Spring to nginx
5. Eventually remove routing logic from Spring Cloud Gateway

